<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Zipazgo of Bacatá</h1><p class="st-meta">1450 CE – 1538 CE (approx.) · capital Bacatá</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The southern Muisca realm of the zipa, centred on the savanna of Bogotá, the most powerful native state in Colombia when the Spanish arrived.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Bacatá, on the high savanna where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Bogotá</a> now stands, became the seat of the zipa, ruler of the southern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca</a>, in the 15th century. Muisca tradition names Meicuchuca as an early zipa, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saguamanchica">Saguamanchica</a> expanded the realm by conquering neighbouring chiefdoms such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fusagasuga">Fusagasugá</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The zipa ruled through subordinate chiefs who paid tribute and supplied warriors. Succession went from uncle to nephew, and heirs were trained in seclusion. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemequene">Nemequene</a>, who reigned in the early 16th century, is credited with a code of laws. The realm controlled the salt mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zipaquira">Zipaquirá</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemocon">Nemocón</a>, whose salt was traded widely. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-guatavita">Lake Guatavita</a>, a sacred lake, lay within its territory. Wars with the zaque of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zacazgo-of-hunza">Zacazgo of Hunza</a> were frequent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonzalo-jimenez-de-quesada">Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada</a> entered the savanna in 1537. The zipa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisquesusa">Tisquesusa</a> died fighting that year, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagipa">Sagipa</a> died in Spanish captivity in 1539 after being tortured for gold. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a> was founded in 1538 as capital of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/new-kingdom-of-granada">New Kingdom of Granada</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1450 CE – 1538 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South America</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:88.56%;width:1.75%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1450 CE – 1538 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bacatá</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Saguamanchica, Nemequene, Tisquesusa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Muisca religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Muisca (Chibcha)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/colombia">Colombia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bacatá, Zipazgo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1494&amp;focus=zipazgo-of-bacata" aria-label="Zipazgo of Bacatá on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2638 2049 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Zipazgo of Bacatá lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2942 2257h0M4910 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2958 2305h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2958 2305h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Colombia. <a href="/atlas?year=1494&amp;focus=zipazgo-of-bacata">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

